# world-holidays

Public holidays for **every country**, for the current and next year.
Zero runtime dependencies, fully typed, works in Node and the browser.

[![npm](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/world-holidays)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/world-holidays)
[![CI](https://github.com/opurbo007/world-holidays/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/opurbo007/world-holidays/actions/workflows/ci.yml)
[![license](https://img.shields.io/npm/l/world-holidays)](#license)

```bash
npm i world-holidays
```

## Quick start

```ts
import { getHolidays } from "world-holidays";

getHolidays("BD"); // whole current year
getHolidays("BD", 2027); // a specific year
getHolidays("BD", { year: "next" }); // next year
getHolidays("BD", { month: "March" }); // one month of this year
getHolidays("BD", { year: 2027, month: 12 }); // one month of a specific year
getHolidays("BD", { year: "all" }); // every covered year
```

Every option is optional. Each holiday looks like this:

```ts
{ date: "2026-12-16", name: "Victory Day", weekday: "Wednesday" }
```

Dates are `YYYY-MM-DD` in the country's local calendar, so there are no timezone surprises.

## Find a country

You don't need to know the ISO code. Search by code or part of the name:

```ts
import { searchCountries, getCountries } from "world-holidays";

searchCountries("bangla"); // [{ code: "BD", name: "People's Republic of Bangladesh" }]
getCountries(); // all 207 countries as { code, name }[]
```

## More examples

```ts
import {
  getMonthHolidays,
  getYearHolidays,
  isHoliday,
  getNextHoliday,
  getUpcomingHolidays,
  getWorldHolidays,
  getAllHolidaysFlat,
} from "world-holidays";

getYearHolidays("JP", "next"); // shortcut for a year
getMonthHolidays("US", "dec", 2026); // shortcut for a month

isHoliday("US", "2026-12-25"); // { date, name: "Christmas Day", weekday: "Friday" }
isHoliday("US", new Date()); // holiday object, or null

getNextHoliday("BD"); // next holiday from today
getNextHoliday("BD", "2026-11-06"); // next holiday from a date
getUpcomingHolidays("GB", 3); // next 3 holidays from today

getWorldHolidays({ year: 2026, month: "December" }); // every country, grouped
getAllHolidaysFlat({ year: 2027, month: "January" }); // one flat list, sorted by date
```

## Per-country import (small bundles)

`import ... from "world-holidays"` includes all 207 countries (about 250 KB, 26 KB gzipped).
If your frontend needs one country, import just that one:

```ts
import { country } from "world-holidays/country/BD";

country.getHolidays({ month: "December" });
country.getNextHoliday();
country.getUpcomingHolidays(5);
```

A single-country bundle is about **3 KB** (1.3 KB gzipped).

- Use the **uppercase** country code in the path: `/country/BD`, not `/country/bd`.
  Linux servers and Vercel are case-sensitive.
- There is also a default export: `import bd from "world-holidays/country/BD"`.
- CommonJS: `const { country } = require("world-holidays/country/BD")`.

The country object has the same methods as the main package, without the country argument:
`getHolidays`, `getYearHolidays`, `getMonthHolidays`, `isHoliday`, `getNextHoliday`, `getUpcomingHolidays`.

### Example: Next.js server component

```tsx
import { country } from "world-holidays/country/BD";

export default function UpcomingHolidays() {
  const list = country.getUpcomingHolidays(5);

  return (
    <ul>
      {list.map((h) => (
        <li key={h.date}>
          {h.date} ({h.weekday}): {h.name}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

## API

All functions that take a country accept the ISO code, case-insensitive (`"bd"` and `"BD"` both work).

| Function                                   | Returns                                                                    |
| ------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `getHolidays(code, options?)`              | Holidays of one country. `options` is `{ year?, month? }` or a year number |
| `getYearHolidays(code, year?)`             | Whole year (default: current year)                                         |
| `getMonthHolidays(code, month, year?)`     | One month (default: current year)                                          |
| `isHoliday(code, date?)`                   | The holiday on that date, or `null`                                        |
| `getNextHoliday(code, from?)`              | The next holiday on or after a date, or `null`                             |
| `getUpcomingHolidays(code, limit?, from?)` | The next `limit` holidays (default 5)                                      |
| `getWorldHolidays(options?)`               | Every country with its holidays                                            |
| `getAllHolidaysFlat(options?)`             | One flat list across all countries, sorted by date                         |
| `getCountries()`                           | All supported countries                                                    |
| `searchCountries(query)`                   | Countries matching a code or part of a name                                |
| `years`                                    | Years covered by this version, e.g. `[2026, 2027]`                         |
| `generatedAt`                              | When the data was generated (ISO string)                                   |

### Options

| Option  | Values                                           | Default        |
| ------- | ------------------------------------------------ | -------------- |
| `year`  | a number, `"current"`, `"next"`, or `"all"`      | `"current"`    |
| `month` | `1`-`12`, or a name such as `"March"` or `"mar"` | the whole year |

### Errors

Bad input throws a clear error instead of returning wrong data:

- Unknown country code: `Error`, with a hint to use `searchCountries()`.
- A year that is not covered: `RangeError`, listing the available years.
- An invalid month: `RangeError`.

### TypeScript

Types are included: `Holiday`, `Country`, `CountryHolidays`, `FlatHoliday`, `CountryCalendar`, `HolidayOptions`, `YearInput`, `MonthInput`.

## Good to know

- **Only official public holidays** are included. Bank holidays, observances and optional days are left out.
- **Substitute days are included.** For example, the UK returns `Boxing Day (substitute day)` when Boxing Day falls on a weekend. Filter on the name if you don't want them.
- **Dates are calculated from rules**, not copied from government announcements.
  Holidays based on moon sighting (for example Eid) can differ by a day.
  Always verify critical dates against an official source.
- **Coverage is two years**: the year the data was generated and the next one.
  `year: "current"` keeps working for the whole of the covered years, even if you don't update the package.

## How the data stays fresh

The holiday data is generated at build time from [date-holidays](https://github.com/commenthol/date-holidays).
A scheduled GitHub Action runs monthly, pulls the newest rules, regenerates the data, and publishes a new
version only when something changed. On 1 January the covered years move forward automatically.

To regenerate locally:

```bash
npm install
npm run build   # generates data, builds the package
npm test
```

## Contributing

Issues and pull requests are welcome. If a holiday is wrong or missing, please include the country,
the year, and an official source. Data fixes usually belong upstream in
[date-holidays](https://github.com/commenthol/date-holidays), and they reach this package on the next update.

